{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It requires evaluating the outcomes of both lotteries and determining whether their differences are considered negligible, then comparing the probabilities of obtaining a favorable outcome. In Lottery A, the possible outcomes are 5000 Euros (0.1%) and 0 Euros (99.9%). In Lottery B, the outcome is 5 Euros with 100% probability. The outcome difference (5000 vs. 5 Euros) is substantial in absolute terms, but the decision reason assumes these are considered negligible—this is a subjective assumption inherent to the reason itself. Under this assumption, the 'favorable outcome' is treated as any positive outcome, regardless of amount. Thus, Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of a favorable outcome (5000 Euros), and Lottery B has a 100% chance of a favorable outcome (5 Euros). The reason would prefer Lottery B due to its higher probability of a positive outcome, assuming outcome differences are negligible.",
